    <node id="Paper_423">
      <data key="d0">Paper</data>
      <data key="d1">Vis</data>
      <data key="d2">2002</data>
      <data key="d3">CPR - curved planar reformation</data>
      <data key="d4">10.1109/visual.2002.1183754</data>
      <data key="d5">http://dx.doi.org/10.1109/VISUAL.2002.1183754</data>
      <data key="d6">37.0</data>
      <data key="d7">44.0</data>
      <data key="d8">C</data>
      <data key="d9">Visualization of tubular structures such as blood vessels is an important topic in medical imaging. One way to display tubular structures for diagnostic purposes is to generate longitudinal cross-sections in order to show their lumen, wall, and surrounding tissue in a curved plane. This process is called curved planar reformation (CPR). We present three different methods to generate CPR images. A tube-phantom was scanned with computed tomography (CT) to illustrate the properties of the different CPR methods. Furthermore we introduce enhancements to these methods: thick-CPR, rotating-CPR and multi-path-CPR.</data>
      <data key="d10">Armin Kanitsar;Dominik Fleischmann;Rainer Wegenkittl;Petr Felkel;Meister Eduard Gröller</data>
      <data key="d11">A. Kanitsar;D. Fleischmann;R. Wegenkittl;P. Felkel;E. Groller</data>
      <data key="d12">Institute of Computer Graphics and Algorithms, University of Technology, Vienna, Austria;Department of Radiology, University of Technology, Vienna, Austria;VRVis Research Center, Vienna, Austria;VRVis Research Center, Vienna, Austria;Institute of Computer Graphics and Algorithms, University of Technology, Vienna, Austria</data>
      <data key="d13">10.1109/visual.2001.964555;10.1109/visual.2001.964538</data>
      <data key="d14">computed tomography angiography, vessel analysis, curved planar reformation</data>
      <data key="d15">286.0</data>
      <data key="d16">68.0</data>
      <data key="d17">14.0</data>
      <data key="d18">976.0</data>
      <data key="d19" />
      <data key="d20" />
    </node>
    <node id="Author_Dominik Fleischmann">
      <data key="d0">Author</data>
      <data key="d21">Dominik Fleischmann</data>
