    <node id="Paper_2915">
      <data key="d0">Paper</data>
      <data key="d1">Vis</data>
      <data key="d2">1996</data>
      <data key="d3">Clinical evaluation of interactive volume visualization</data>
      <data key="d4">10.1109/visual.1996.568134</data>
      <data key="d5">http://dx.doi.org/10.1109/VISUAL.1996.568134</data>
      <data key="d6">367.0</data>
      <data key="d7">370.0</data>
      <data key="d8">C</data>
      <data key="d9">The case study describes a system that allows the use of interactive volume rendering for routine clinical diagnosis. In this setup, a SGI RealityStation acts like a remote rendering system which is controlled by a user interface that was added to an existing clinical system. The paper describes some implementation aspects, including several system optimizations that were carried out in order to optimize rendering speed. Initial results are very promising; the authors present three examples of clinical findings that were made using this system. Because of the setup, clinicians are now much more aware of the possibilities that modern hardware offers for interactive volume visualization.</data>
      <data key="d10">Karel J. Zuiderveld;Peter M. A. van Ooijen;John W. C. Chin-A-Woeng;Pieter C. Buijs;Marco Olree;Frits H. Post</data>
      <data key="d11">K.J. Zuiderveld;P.M.A. van Ooijen;J.W.C. Chin-A-Woeng;P.C. Buijs;M. Olree;F.H. Posti</data>
      <data key="d12">Institute of Medical Image Science, University Hospital Utrecht, Netherlands;Department of Technical Mathematics and Informatics, Delft University of Technnology, Netherlands;Department of Technical Mathematics and Informatics, Delft University of Technnology, Netherlands;Institute of Medical Image Science, University Hospital Utrecht, Netherlands;Institute of Medical Image Science, University Hospital Utrecht, Netherlands;Department of Technical Mathematics and Informatics, Delft University of Technnology, Netherlands</data>
      <data key="d13" />
      <data key="d14">Volume Rendering, Texture Mapping, MR Angiography, Visualization, Clinical Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d15">2.0</data>
      <data key="d16">4.0</data>
      <data key="d17">4.0</data>
      <data key="d18">52.0</data>
      <data key="d19" />
      <data key="d20" />
    </node>
