    </node>
    <node id="Paper_3672">
      <data key="d0">Paper</data>
      <data key="d1">VAST</data>
      <data key="d2">2007</data>
      <data key="d3">University of British Columbia &amp; Simon Fraser University - The Bricolage</data>
      <data key="d4">10.1109/vast.2007.4389020</data>
      <data key="d5">http://dx.doi.org/10.1109/VAST.2007.4389020</data>
      <data key="d6">207.0</data>
      <data key="d7">208.0</data>
      <data key="d8">M</data>
      <data key="d9">This abstract presents a&lt;i&gt;bricolage&lt;/i&gt;approach to the 2007 VAST contest. The analytical process we used is presented across four stages of sensemaking. Several tools were used throughout our approach, and we present their strengths and weaknesses for specific aspects of the analytical process. In addition, we review the details of both individual and collaborative techniques for solving visual analytics problems.</data>
      <data key="d10">William Chao;Daniel Ha;Kevin Ho;Linda T. Kaastra;Minjung Kim;Andrew Wade</data>
      <data key="d11">William Chao;Daniel Ha;Kevin Ho;Linda Kaastra;Minjung Kim;Andrew Wade;Brian Fisher</data>
      <data key="d12">MAGIC, SIAT and University of British Columbia, Canada;MAGIC, SIAT and University of British Columbia, Canada;MAGIC, SIAT and University of British Columbia, Canada;MAGIC, SIAT and University of British Columbia, Canada;MAGIC, SIAT and University of British Columbia, Canada;MAGIC, SIAT and University of British Columbia, Canada;MAGIC, SIAT and University of British Columbia</data>
